{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target concedes the non-experts may 'reach the right answer' (via brittle compliance that tracks correctness), yet never identifies what within-evaluation evidence would distinguish that mere-compliance case from a genuine reliability gain — the exact second task the problem demands. Its only observation-type counterconditions address when brittle compliance won't occur, not how to tell compliance-with-correct-answer apart from true reliability within a single evaluation.","counterexample":[{"evaluation_observation":"For a verifiable task, the panel and the expert disagree on only edge-case items, the panel's per-item justifications quote the checklist verbatim and stop when the item is satisfied, the expert cites case-specific reasons the item is misleading yet arrives at the same outcome, and both sets of answers are scored correct at equal rates.","fault":"The target's 'right answer for the wrong reasons' framing predicts this pattern but supplies no evidence criterion to separate checklist-compliance-that-tracks-correctness from a reliability gain, so under its own observation counterconditions it cannot adjudicate the exact distinction the problem requires."}],"target_alias":"SRC_001"}]}